welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and today our  guest is Professor David skelly a  professor of ecology at Yale's  environment school professor skelly is  interested in animal ecology  conservation and management his studies  of amphibians are directed at  determining the causes of patterns such  as evolution and the extinction and  establishment of populations today we're  going to talk to Professor skelly about  rapid evolution the idea that evolution  can keep pace with environmental change  welcome professor Skelly thank you much  of your research is focused on  amphibians how did you become interested  in them well I went to graduate school  thinking that I was going to study fish  I had been interested in fish for much  of my childhood and when I got to the  University of Michigan where I went to  graduate school my advisor had switched  to amphibians and so I started my first  study with amphibians and have learned  that they're very useful group to study  your latest research looks at species  when they invade a new environment tell  me about your research in Australia with  the cane toads well this work is a  collaboration with some colleagues at  the University of Sydney who have been  watching the cane toads move across the  Australian continent they were  introduced intentionally to try to  control pests in 1935 and they kind of  failed in that assignment and then they  overstayed their welcome and they've  been spreading across the continent and  they're there a problem because they're  they're toxic and in being toxic they  get picked up by native predators by  people's dogs in their backyards and  those animals that try to eat them off  and die so Australians care a great deal  about cane toads there's a very amusing  documentary movie that's been made about  their spread but seriously they're a big  problem so one of the big questions is  how far are they going to get and how  asked we we tackled that problem and  came up with a kind of a surprising  answer and that is that they were going  to go a lot farther than people had  originally suspected and that that  garnered a great deal of attention in  Australia and elsewhere where people  think about invasive species the reason  that was interesting is because it looks  like the reason they're moving so far so  fast is because since nineteen  thirty-five they've evolved and that's  really surprising okay so what does this  mean for the management of the species  when it's introduced well that's a good  question what it means essentially is  that managers and policymakers that are  trying to monitor and make decisions  about how animals move across  international borders across oceans need  to consider whether that animal is going  to be the same in the new country as it  was in its home country and the emerging  answer is that we can't count on that so  the tried and true method of doing some  field trials and saying well this thing  lives on this in this kind of an area  and it eats this kind of a thing we may  not be able to depend on that and so  that means that there should be greater  caution when we're trying to use animals  for good as was the case with a cane  toad trying to control a pest it wasn't  able to do that very well and then it's  turned into something else that's living  in environments that no one would have  ever predicted based on where it lived  in in Central and South America Wow so  it could be quite the problem yes okay  the second element of your research  deals with the study of evolution in  response to changing temperature right  what are you working on now well so our  other rapid evolution project is looking  at a North American species and this is  one of the first times in my research  career that I've worked on what happens  to local populations with ramifications  for thinking about a global phenomenon  most of the time ecologists if asked  what's going to matter for my population  would start giving you answers that are  based on local factors well there's it's  it's sunnier here or at shady or here  wetter  I or whatever and usually that's that's  based on local habitat differences but  now for the first time with climate  change ecologists are forced to deal  with the idea that what may matter most  for their population or certainly may  matter a lot is something that's  happening on a much bigger scale than  their populations exist on so in this  case we're working with North American  species that live in a whole variety of  environments they live in deeply shaded  ponds these are amphibians that breed in  the water and then emerge onto the land  but they have to go through this life  history stage in these ponds and the the  ponds can be either cool and shady or  warm and sunny and so we're using this  as a surrogate for understanding how  these same types of animals and other  animals may evolve in response to  climate change so we've got cool  conditions and warm conditions that can  change very fast just as the trees grow  up or they get cut down or hurricane  knocks them down that all happens on the  scale of decades the same kind of scale  that we think climate change is going to  happen on these species it turns out  respond evolutionarily to these changing  temperature conditions just as the  environment changes at the same pace as  far as we can tell so that the animals  that live in these cool shady conditions  are actually able to do a few tricks  they choose the tadpoles choose where  they're going to live based on  temperature so they actually seek out  little Sun flecks in the pond to raise  their body temperature which helps them  grow and develop faster they have a  higher critical thermal maximum that  means that they can survive warmer  temperatures if they live in warmer  environments and cooler ones if they  live in cooler environments and they  also will develop faster in colder  environments if that's where they live  and these differences exist on on a  stone's throw sort of a distance scale  so a shady pond right next to a sunny  pond will have frog populations that  have evolved those local conditions this  is really surprising to evolutionary  biologists and it's dumbfounding to  conservation biologists even though  conservation biology has biology in its  name and even though the cornerstone of  biology is evolutionary by  LG most conservation scientists don't  think about evolution at all they've if  they've learned anything at all about it  they've learned that it's too slow to  matter and what we're discovering with  this study the cane toad study and  others is that it's typical probably in  nature for evolution to be just as fast  as environmental change that doesn't  mean these are big huge changes it's not  one species turning into another but  they're big enough to matter they're  going to change how these actors in the  environment behave and they're going to  change how they interact with others  it's going to change the ecology so it  does matter so why is it important to  understand how global climate change can  affect species well the temperature  changes that we're looking at on the  order of 3 or 4 degrees Celsius are the  same changes that are expected to happen  over the same half century or so and and  the the temporal scale of the the trees  falling back or growing up is about the  same as well so we're looking at a  phenomenon that's happening at the same  scales that global climate change is  presumed to happen and temperatures  temperatures so if they can evolve  rapidly in response to the kinds of  temperature changes we're looking at  we're fairly confident that they're  going to evolve in response to these  other changes now there's two ways to  view this relative to climate change on  the one hand people who want to argue  that it doesn't matter can say well so  species are going to be able to evolve  there was a study that came out a few  years ago that said up to a third of all  species are going to go extinct because  of climate change well our group would  argue that that number may be a bit high  and that a great many more species  instead of going extinct are going to  undergo natural selection anything that  can threaten a species with extinction  is altering survivorship and growth and  fecundity of that species enough to also  pose a selection event and so many more  species are going to change now some of  these changes can be viewed as an impact  of climate change as well so some  fraction of species are going to go  extinct some greater fraction are going  to be different because of climate  change and some of those differences are  of concern all by them  selection often tends to narrow genetic  diversity and as we narrow this kind of  biological diversity the ability of  these species to deal with future  challenges may be compromised the other  thing is that we want to conserve  biological diversity in general and we  you know conservation biology is this  kind of melange of societal values and  hard-nosed science and our job is  scientist is to provide the scientific  information but the people who get  emotional about this stuff and there are  many of them want things to be as they  were now whether that's realistic or not  that's what they're concerned about and  what we're showing is that even species  that don't go extinct are going to be  fundamentally changed because of climate  change many of them you have said that  the reason we get sick is because the  environmental context has changed and  that the way we change the environment  may come back to change us can you  elaborate on that sure some of our  research is in the ecology of disease  disease ecology is somewhat different  than medicine in that in medicine it's  been traditional to focus on the patient  and to learn what the symptoms are and  then treat those symptoms disease  ecology is a perspective that's focused  on why plants and animals including  humans but but in this case nonhumans  why they get sick and you often the the  kinds of things that make animals sick  are around in the environment all the  time and so there there are pathogens  out there that are just there all the  time but what what happens to make an  individual sick is that the environment  challenge challenges you somehow and may  weaken your ability to resist disease  many people will have anecdotes where  they feel like they got knocked down by  something and and while they weren't  feeling well because of that they got  infected those anecdotes are probably  based on real real things that happen to  people in many cases and the concern  with global climate change  that we're going to be putting perhaps  more people are certainly different  groups of people in situations where  they're going to be challenged by the  environment and in so doing we're going  to subject some populations to greater  likelihood of disease and those diseases  are selection events and humans are  still evolving to deal with those so  that's where that quotation comes from  do you think we should be concerned  about rapid evolution I think we should  be focused on rapid evolution in the  sense that it's something that's real  and it matters and the way that I can  bring that home to people is you think  about the flu vaccine that you got this  year when we think about medical  intervention people don't think twice  about how evolution figures into the way  we live our lives but there's a group of  people out there every year that try to  forecast the evolution of the flu virus  they look at what flu was around where  last year which ones are growing which  ones are shrinking and that's how they  make up the cocktail that we get  injected into our arms every year so in  certain contexts it's not controversial  and it's totally under people's radar  screens but when we try more more  broadly to say evolution matters and we  need to deal with it I think what we  need to do with the public is be more  specific how do we deal with this once  we establish that rapid evolution is  something that we can expect how do we  use that knowledge in a sense to try to  have evolution work for us or at least  not against us a great example is the  way we harvest fishery stocks both in  freshwater and marine environments for  most of the history of managed fisheries  we've taken the biggest individuals and  over time what we're causing is fish to  mature it's smaller and smaller sizes  and so the average fish that hits your  dinner plate is getting smaller and  smaller and that has its own  ramifications for the species survival  in the wild it also influences the  economics of the whole thing as well so  it really matters in that sense and  finally  this is being recognized and we're now  managing fisheries stocks in more  sophisticated ways that may allow fish  to become bigger and to preserve brute  stocks that will will keep fish larger  and potentially health or healthier  better able to resist predators and so  on that's a great example that can be  extended to the way that we move species  around when we're going to reintroduce  species in the wild we should be  thinking about the genetic stock that  they came from and and maybe even  forecast the way the environment is  going to change before we move something  into the environment there are many  examples like this where rapid evolution  can just help us do a better job thanks  very much for being here today with us  and sharing some of your research thank  you for more information about professor  skelly and his fascinating research  please visit our website at yale edu  backslash mcmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  McMillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies  you

